        "test_procedure": "1. Locate the _fft_field function (SG-003) in the codebase.\n2. Identify the pre-condition check: len(vals) MUST be a power of 2 AND len(vals) MUST equal len(roots_of_unity).\n3. CRITICAL: Verify these checks occur BEFORE any FFT butterfly operations begin.\n4. Design falsification tests for non-power-of-2 lengths:\n   (a) len(vals) = 3 (prime, not power of 2)\n   (b) len(vals) = 5 (prime, not power of 2)\n   (c) len(vals) = 6 (composite, not power of 2)\n   (d) len(vals) = 7 (prime, not power of 2)\n   (e) len(vals) = 9 (composite, not power of 2)\n   (f) len(vals) = 15 (one less than power of 2)\n   (g) len(vals) = 17 (one more than power of 2)\n5. Design falsification tests for mismatched lengths:\n   (a) len(vals) = 8, len(roots_of_unity) = 16 (roots larger)\n   (b) len(vals) = 16, len(roots_of_unity) = 8 (vals larger)\n   (c) len(vals) = 8, len(roots_of_unity) = 7 (both different, neither power of 2)\n6. Verify rejection behavior: each invalid input must be rejected with an appropriate error, NOT silently accepted.\n7. Check for silent corruption: if validation is missing, FFT butterfly operations on non-power-of-2 arrays produce incorrect results.\n8. Trace callers: identify all functions that call _fft_field and verify they always provide power-of-2 sized arrays.\n9. Check roots_of_unity source: verify roots_of_unity is always generated with power-of-2 length matching vals.\n10. Test edge cases for valid power-of-2 lengths:\n   (a) len(vals) = 1 (2^0)\n   (b) len(vals) = 2 (2^1)\n   (c) len(vals) = 4096 (typical EIP-4844 value)\n   (d) len(vals) = 8192 (next power of 2)\n11. Verify is_power_of_2 check implementation: common patterns include (n & (n-1)) == 0 for n > 0.\n12. Check for off-by-one: ensure len(vals) = 0 is handled (0 is technically power of 2 by some definitions but may be invalid).\n13. Impact analysis: if non-power-of-2 inputs are accepted, butterfly indices would access wrong elements, producing garbage polynomial evaluations.\n14. Cross-reference with polynomial commitment scheme: FFT outputs feed into KZG commitment generation - corrupted FFT breaks all downstream proofs.\n15. Verify the invariant SG-003:INV-001 is enforced: the function must guarantee power-of-2 constraint holds for all accepted inputs.",
        "bug_class": "Pre-condition Validation / Mathematical Invariant Violation",
        "risk_category": "Tampering",
        "notes": "Source: PROP-W5B14-29, Invariant: SG-003:INV-001, Primary Element: SG-003:_fft_field, Covered Nodes: actor-validator-node. FFT correctness fundamentally requires power-of-2 array sizes. Non-power-of-2 inputs cause the butterfly operation indices to access incorrect elements, propagating errors to all polynomial evaluations, cell generation, and KZG proofs. While internal-only, a bug here would corrupt the entire cryptographic pipeline. The test should attempt to FALSIFY the pre-condition by finding code paths that bypass the power-of-2 check.",
